From 5dd1028cb01192f7b5be56801e0a75f1e444599a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: KEX patch <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 15:07:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] KEX: four views in a 2x2 grid, snap-out windows, built-in KEX
 suite

Four views, snap-out view windows and plugin gating for two-view
plugins. The KEX suite (PowerEditor/src/KexSuite, copied in by
kex-patch.sh) is built into notepad++.exe. It has a menu of its own in
the menu bar, KEX, between Plugins and Window (MENUINDEX_KEX; Window and
? move one place right, and the translation positions with them), whose
Settings... opens the suite's own KEX Settings window, apart from
Notepad++'s Preferences.

Tab snapping: while a tab is dragged the core works out where it would
land (the side of a view a hidden view would open on, or another view)
and the suite draws the preview; with it on, a drop goes there instead
of showing the Move/Clone menu.

A Notepad++ for each virtual desktop: a second notepad++.exe started on
a desktop that has no Notepad++ window becomes a new Notepad++ there,
opening with a copy of the first window's tabs, carets and active tabs
(NPPM_KEX_SAVEDESKTOPCOPY / NPPM_KEX_LOADDESKTOPCOPY). Windows never
move between desktops.

A tab drag starts from anywhere on the tab once the mouse has moved the
system drag distance, and tells the suite so it can lift the tab.

The app calls itself "Notepad++ KEX" in the title bar, the About box,
the debug info and the version resource (the taskbar and Task Manager
name); the window class stays "Notepad++". The Settings menu and the
Preferences dialog are as upstream has them; NPPM_KEX_SHOWPREFPAGE
opens the KEX Settings window on a given page.

Scintilla draws the suite's motion itself, from pictures of the view:
the tab slide (SCI_KEX_SLIDE) and the motion of edits
(SCI_KEX_SETMOTIONS): cut, delete, paste, undo and redo, moving,
indenting, duplicating, folding and sorting lines, a ring closing onto
found text and a slide for far jumps. The view is pictured the moment
an edit starts; each frame then puts the view as it is now together
with that picture, so text stays sharp and nothing floats over other
text. Each motion has its own time; any key, other edit, scroll or new
size ends it at once, and one that comes right after another is skipped.

